{"agent":"bugs","findings":[{"id":"bugs-001","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":233,"title":"Reverts key-leak fix: raw HTTPStatusError string (full request URL) logged, bypassing _sanitize_exc data-exposure control","category":"error-handling","cwe":"CWE-532","severity":"medium","agent_confidence":72,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"The diff swaps `status=exc.response.status_code` for `exc=exc`. That undoes commit d47b4c2 ("fix(security): prevent potential key leakage in validate_connection log messages"). str(httpx.HTTPStatusError) contains the full request URL: "Client error '404 Not Found' for url 'http://host/api/v3/system/status'...". The rest of the codebase deliberately does not stringify httpx exceptions. engine.py:30-44 `_sanitize_exc` and scheduler.py:218-224 and 400-403 route them through a type-based summary because the URL can carry credentials that the loguru redacting sink does not know about. The `apikey=` query parameter is blocked by models/config.py:67-87, but userinfo credentials such as http://user:pass@host (reverse-proxy basic auth) are not in collect_secrets. They would now reach the logs and the in-memory log buffer shown in the UI. The status_code-only version held this guarantee on every path; the new version depends on the redacting sink knowing every secret. The same method's _request_with_retry (lines 71-75) still uses the sanitized form, so the two paths are now inconsistent.","current_code":"logger.warning(
    "{app}: Unexpected HTTP error: {exc}",
    app=self._app_name,
    exc=exc,
)","fix_hint":"restore status-code-only logging, or route through engine._sanitize_exc as scheduler.py does","why_it_matters":"URL-embedded credentials (userinfo, proxy auth) that the redaction list does not cover are written to the logs and to the UI log view.","silenced_marker_nearby":false},{"id":"bugs-002","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":252,"title":"Reverts sanitization: raw pydantic ValidationError (including response input values) logged","category":"error-handling","cwe":"CWE-532","severity":"medium","agent_confidence":62,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"The diff replaces `count=exc.error_count()` with `exc=exc`. str(pydantic.ValidationError) includes `input_value=` for each failed field, which here means the raw /system/status response body echoed into the logs. The project convention (engine.py `_sanitize_exc` returns "validation error (N issues)"; scheduler.py:218-224) explicitly sanitizes pydantic exceptions before logging. This hunk reverts commit d47b4c2, which put the count-only form in place for this exact reason. If the configured URL points at a proxy or login page that returns a JSON body with tokens or session data, that content lands in the logs unredacted. pending: confirm what non-arr bodies at the status path could contain sensitive values.","current_code":"logger.warning(
    "{app}: Unexpected API response format: {exc}",
    app=self._app_name,
    exc=exc,
)","fix_hint":"restore exc.error_count() or use _sanitize_exc(exc)","why_it_matters":"Echoing arbitrary upstream response content into logs defeats the codebase's established exception-sanitization boundary.","silenced_marker_nearby":false}],"agent_notes":["git log -S shows commit d47b4c2 "fix(security): prevent potential key leakage in validate_connection log messages" introduced the exact lines this diff removes. The working-tree change is a straight revert of a security control."]}

{"agent":"architecture","findings":[{"id":"arch-001","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":233,"title":"validate_connection now logs the raw HTTPStatusError, bypassing the codebase's established exception-sanitization pattern","category":"pattern-consistency","cwe":null,"severity":"medium","agent_confidence":88,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"The codebase already handles this problem one way: exceptions from *arr HTTP calls are reduced to a safe summary before logging. That summary is `f"HTTP {exc.response.status_code}"` for status errors and `type(exc).__name__` otherwise. You can see this in triggarr/clients/base.py:71-75 (the retry path in this same class), in triggarr/tracking.py:70-74, and in `_sanitize_exc` in triggarr/search/engine.py:30-44, which is used at engine.py:366, 619, 881, 1138, 1274 and 1413. This diff swaps the old status-code-only log for the raw `exc`. str(HTTPStatusError) includes the full request URL, query string included, and a link to MDN. `_sanitize_exc`'s docstring says this kind of raw output is what the helper exists to prevent.","current_code":"logger.warning(
                    "{app}: Unexpected HTTP error: {exc}",
                    app=self._app_name,
                    exc=exc,
                )","fix_hint":"revert to status_code-only, or move `_sanitize_exc` somewhere shared (e.g. triggarr/clients/base.py or a small util) and call it here so clients and engine share one sanitizer","why_it_matters":"ArrClient is now inconsistent with itself: the retry path at line 71 sanitizes and validate_connection at line 233 does not. Raw exception text can include configured URLs and internal hosts. The loguru redacting sink only scrubs known secret values, so anything else in that text reaches the log buffer that the web UI shows.","silenced_marker_nearby":false,"needs_verification":null},{"id":"arch-002","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":252,"title":"validate_connection now logs the full pydantic ValidationError instead of the established error-count summary","category":"pattern-consistency","cwe":null,"severity":"medium","agent_confidence":85,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"The established convention for pydantic.ValidationError is to log only the error count: `_sanitize_exc` in triggarr/search/engine.py:42-43 returns `validation error ({exc.error_count()} issues)` and is used at 6 call sites, and tracking.py:70-74 logs only the type name. Before this diff the code here followed that convention. Now it logs str(exc). Pydantic v2 puts `input_value=...` for each failing field into that string, so parts of the remote server's response body (whatever /system/status returned) get echoed into the logs.","current_code":"logger.warning(
                "{app}: Unexpected API response format: {exc}",
                app=self._app_name,
                exc=exc,
            )","fix_hint":"keep `count=exc.error_count()` (or call the shared `_sanitize_exc`), and if field-level detail is wanted, log only the `loc`/`type` of each error, not the `input_value`","why_it_matters":"This is a step back from a sanitization rule applied consistently everywhere else, and it lets content controlled by a remote, possibly misconfigured or hostile endpoint (for example a reverse-proxy login page) into user-visible logs. pending: confirm whether the improved diagnostics were a deliberate trade-off; no intent doc was supplied.","silenced_marker_nearby":false,"needs_verification":null}],"agent_notes":["The diff takes the shape of an intentional revert of the sanitized logging. There is no intent document or commit message saying the more verbose diagnostics are wanted. If they are, the consistent approach is to change `_sanitize_exc` and the two inline copies together, not just validate_connection.","Duplication note, not a finding: the inline `f"HTTP {exc.response.status_code}" if isinstance(exc, httpx.HTTPStatusError) else type(exc).__name__` expression appears in triggarr/clients/base.py:71-75 and triggarr/tracking.py:70-74, and `_sanitize_exc` in triggarr/search/engine.py is a superset of it. That makes three implementations of the same idea, all private to their modules. Moving `_sanitize_exc` into a shared module (e.g. triggarr/clients/base.py or triggarr/logging.py) would make it easy for clients, tracking and engine to stay consistent, and would have made this regression stand out.","`_sanitize_exc`'s docstring says raw str(exc) may 'bypass the loguru redacting sink'. triggarr/logging.py redacts the full formatted output, so configured secrets are still scrubbed. The remaining risk is non-secret but sensitive content (URLs, internal hosts, response bodies). The API key is sent in the X-Api-Key header, not the URL, so str(HTTPStatusError) should not contain it directly.","No tests assert on the 'Unexpected HTTP error' or 'Unexpected API response format' messages (grep of tests/), so this change is untested in both directions. Neither the old nor the new log format is pinned.","No new imports, dependencies, cycles or cross-module coupling. The change is confined to log-message formatting in ArrClient.validate_connection, and the subclasses (lidarr/radarr/sonarr) inherit it unchanged."]}

{"agent":"impact","findings":[{"id":"impact-1","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":233,"title":"Credential exposure in logs and the web UI log viewer: raw HTTPStatusError text puts the full request URL, including userinfo passwords and ?token= values, into the log output","category":"blast-radius","cwe":"CWE-532","severity":"high","agent_confidence":82,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":"CLAUDE.md (project) Deep Code Review #1: 'No API keys in logs'; user CLAUDE.md: 'Never log sensitive data (passwords, tokens...)'","problem":"The old code logged only `exc.response.status_code`. The diff now logs the whole exception object. In the installed httpx, the HTTPStatusError message is built at .venv/lib/python3.11/site-packages/httpx/_models.py:816 as "... for url '{0.url}'". `httpx.URL.__str__` (_urls.py:374) returns the raw URI with userinfo left unmasked. Only `__repr__` masks it. The client keeps userinfo on request.url, because _client.py:469 reads request.url.username/password to build BasicAuth. Config validation still accepts both leaking shapes. validate_arr_url_config (triggarr/web/validation.py:112-157) checks only the scheme, the hostname and blocked IPs, so userinfo passes. reject_apikey_in_url (triggarr/models/config.py:65-89) rejects only keys that start with `apikey`, and its docstring says `?token=foo` stays valid for reverse-proxy setups. Concrete trigger: an instance url of `https://bob:s3cret@arr.example/` or `https://proxy/sonarr?token=abc` where /api/v3/system/status returns any non-401 error (403 from the proxy, 404 for a wrong subpath, 500 or 502). The call path is startup.py:160 -&gt; validate_connection -&gt; the else branch at base.py:232. The log line then contains `s3cret` or `token=abc`. The redacting sinks (triggarr/logging.py:36-42 and 72-82) replace only values collected by startup.collect_secrets, meaning *arr API keys and auth secrets, so URL passwords and proxy tokens go unredacted to stderr and to log_buffer, which feeds the web UI log viewer. A 3xx response also adds the Location header to the message (_models.py:810-811), so an SSO redirect URL ends up in the log too. The repo already has a helper that guards this exact property, `_sanitize_exc` (triggarr/search/engine.py:30-44), and the scheduler uses it for this reason (scheduler.py:218-234, 400-410). The same file uses the same sanitization in `_request_with_retry` (base.py:71-75). This diff removes that guarantee from the changed line.","current_code":"logger.warning(
    "{app}: Unexpected HTTP error: {exc}",
    app=self._app_name,
    exc=exc,
)","fix_hint":"Restore the status-only message, or log `_sanitize_exc(exc)`. If more detail is needed, add only `exc.response.reason_phrase` and leave the URL out.","why_it_matters":"A basic-auth password or a reverse-proxy token for the *arr instance gets written to persistent container logs and shown in the browser log viewer. This is the class of leak the project's SEC-02 and _sanitize_exc work was built to prevent.","silenced_marker_nearby":false},{"id":"impact-2","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":252,"title":"Data exposure: raw pydantic ValidationError text writes the *arr status response payload (input_value) to logs and the web UI","category":"blast-radius","cwe":"CWE-532","severity":"medium","agent_confidence":62,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":"triggarr/search/engine.py:30-35 _sanitize_exc docstring: avoid raw str(exc), which may contain internal paths/URLs","problem":"`str(pydantic.ValidationError)` includes `input_value=` for each error, which is a truncated repr of the JSON body that was rejected. Before this diff only `exc.error_count()` was logged. Trigger: a reverse proxy, auth gateway or wrong subpath answers /api/v3/system/status (or the Lidarr v1 path) with JSON that is not a SystemStatus, such as an auth-gateway error object or a different app's API. That body, including any session identifiers, internal hostnames or filesystem paths (SystemStatus normally carries appData and startupPath), is written to stderr and to the log_buffer shown in the UI. The redacting sink catches none of it unless it happens to equal an *arr API key. The codebase's own `_sanitize_exc` (engine.py:42-43) reduces this to `validation error (N issues)`, which is exactly what the removed code did. The truncated repr limits the amount leaked, so this is less severe than impact-1, but it is the same control being reverted.","current_code":"logger.warning(
    "{app}: Unexpected API response format: {exc}",
    app=self._app_name,
    exc=exc,
)","fix_hint":"Keep the error count, or log field locations only (`[e['loc'] for e in exc.errors(include_input=False)]`). Do not log `str(exc)`.","why_it_matters":"Upstream response bodies (internal paths, proxy or auth responses) reach operator-visible logs and the authenticated UI log viewer, which goes against the project's sanitization convention.","silenced_marker_nearby":false}],"agent_notes":["Blast radius is small in code and narrow in reach. `validate_connection` has exactly one caller, triggarr/startup.py:160 (validate_connections), which runs once per enabled instance at startup. The Radarr, Sonarr and Lidarr clients all inherit it, so every instance type is affected. Its signature and return value are unchanged, so no importer breaks.","Effects of the change: it only alters log message text. There is no API, schema, migration or performance impact. No test asserts on these log strings (grep of tests/ for 'Unexpected HTTP error' or 'Unexpected API response format' finds nothing), so the suite will pass whether or not the leak is present.","The diff contradicts the codebase's own convention. `_request_with_retry` in the same file (base.py:71-75), `_on_job_error` (scheduler.py:400-410) and the tracking path (scheduler.py:218-234) all deliberately route httpx and pydantic exceptions through `_sanitize_exc` (engine.py:30-44). This diff makes validate_connection the only place that logs raw exception text.","Why the redacting sink doesn't help: logging.py redacts only exact secret strings from startup.collect_secrets (*arr API keys and auth secrets). URL userinfo passwords, proxy `?token=` values (explicitly allowed by config.py:74-75), 3xx Location headers and response bodies are not on that list.","Exposure surfaces: container stderr (docker logs, persisted by the host log driver) and log_buffer, which the web UI log viewer renders to authenticated users.","Verdict: blocking issue. The diff reverts sanitized error logging in validate_connection, which leaks URL credentials. Both hunks should restore the status-only and count-only messages or call `_sanitize_exc`."]}
